          <caption>
            <p>Midline Sagittal Anatomy of the Cranial Dura and Venous Sinuses. This diagram illustrates the falx cerebri, tentorium cerebelli, superior sagittal sinus, straight sinus, crista galli, and pituitary gland in a sagittal plane. Image courtesy O. Chaigasame</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="falx__cerebri" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-36278.s13">
        <fig id="article-36278.image.f2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Superior Sagittal Sinus and Associated Venous Structures. The superior sagittal sinus is a major dural venous sinus located along the midline of the skull within the falx cerebri. The sinus collects venous blood from cerebral veins, dural veins, and venous lacunae. Venous lacunae also facilitate&#x000a0;cerebrospinal fluid absorption via arachnoid granulations. The venous system drains blood from the brain into the systemic circulation. Henry Vandyke Carter, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?search=Gray+Anatomy+566&#x00026;title=Special%3AMediaSearch&#x00026;type=image">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray566" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-36278.s14">
        <fig id="article-36278.image.f3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Sagittal View of Cranial Nerves, Dural Membranes, and Venous Sinuses. This anatomical illustration details the internal structures of the skull, including the falx cerebri, tentorium cerebelli, and diaphragma sellae. The venous system is represented by the superior&#x000a0;and inferior sagittal sinuses, the straight sinus, and the transverse sinus. Labeled nerves include the olfactory tract, optic nerves, oculomotor, trochlear, trigeminal, abducent, facial, acoustic, glossopharyngeal, vagus, and accessory nerves. Vascular and support structures shown include the ophthalmic artery, great cerebral vein, and nerve sheath. Henry Vandyke Carter, <ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gray567.png">Public Domain</ext-link>, via Wikimedia Commons</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="Gray567" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-36278.s15">
        <fig id="article-36278.image.f4" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Falx Cerebri on Computed Tomography, Coronal View. This coronal section of a head computed tomography scan demonstrates the internal structures of the brain and skull. The red arrow identifies the falx cerebri, a dural fold situated within the longitudinal fissure between the cerebral hemispheres. Contributed by Sunil Munakomi, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="FALX" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
      <floats-group id="article-36278.s16">
        <fig id="article-36278.image.f5" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <p>Falx Cerebri on Computed Tomography, Axial View. This axial-view radiographic image highlights the interhemispheric fissure. The red arrow identifies the falx cerebri, a sickle-shaped dural fold that is clearly visible as a linear density at this level of the cranial vault. Contributed by Sunil Munakomi, MD</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" xlink:href="FALX3" position="float" orientation="portrait"/>
        </fig>
      </floats-group>
